WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:16.680
Hey there, welcome to the Posit Data Science Hangout. I'm Libby Herron, and this is a recording

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:22.040
of our weekly community call that happens every Thursday at 12pm US Eastern Time. If

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:27.280
you are not joining us live, you miss out on the amazing chat that's going on. So find

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:30.719
the link in the description where you can add our call to your calendar and come hang

00:00:30.719 --> 00:00:36.320
out with the most supportive, friendly, and funny data community you'll ever experience.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:40.599
Can't wait to see you there. And speaking of people who make impact, I would really

00:00:40.599 --> 00:00:46.180
like to introduce you to our featured leader today, Vytas Vaiciulis. He is Lead Data Scientist

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:51.000
at CSO, which is Central Statistics Office Ireland. Vytas, thank you so much for joining

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:55.139
us today. And I would love it if you could introduce yourself, tell us a little bit about

00:00:55.139 --> 00:00:59.900
what you do at CSO, and then also something that you like to do for fun.

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:06.099
Thank you very much. So very happy to be here. So I'm originally from Lithuania, but I've

00:01:06.099 --> 00:01:11.760
been living in Ireland for more than 30 years. And what do you do at CSO? So I lead a section

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:17.779
on analytics and AI innovations, which I'm very proud to say, when I began working in

00:01:17.779 --> 00:01:24.220
that section, it was team of one, and now it's team of 13. So I build it from the ground

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:31.260
up, and the entire idea of the section was to modernize our statistics, which I have

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:36.620
done, and I'm very proud of it. And that's where my interaction with POSIT comes in,

00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:42.260
and our studio. So that's kind of like my professional work. For fun, I have access

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:47.379
to the Atlantic Ocean. So I go for swims in the Atlantic Oceans, and in my spare time,

00:01:47.379 --> 00:01:55.300
I lift heavy objects, and I put them back down. That keeps me sane, because it frees

00:01:55.300 --> 00:02:00.599
up the mind from my day-to-day thinking, because in data science, there's a lot of thinking,

00:02:00.599 --> 00:02:03.699
so sometimes I want to forget a little bit, and de-stress.

00:02:03.699 --> 00:02:08.940
I don't think you're the only lifter here. I used to lift too. You're in good company.

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:15.740
All right, everybody, get your questions for Vitus ready. Isabella is going to share Slido

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:20.899
links again in the chat so that you know where to ask questions. But I would like to

00:02:20.899 --> 00:02:27.059
ask you, Vitus, how did you get to Ireland? How did you get to CSL?

00:02:27.059 --> 00:02:32.860
So I suppose, initially, it was my family. So when I was 13 years old, my parents moved

00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:38.059
to Ireland. And when I was in Lithuania one of the days, they rang me up and said, son,

00:02:38.059 --> 00:02:43.979
we're moving to Ireland. I was, OK. So that's how I initially moved to Ireland. Long story

00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:50.779
short, after that, I did all of my education in Ireland. I went to school, which was a very

00:02:50.779 --> 00:02:57.339
new experience. And then after I finished up my university, I was applying for jobs.

00:02:57.339 --> 00:03:03.339
And by pure chance, I ended up in CSO, Central Statistics Office. And I've been always working

00:03:03.339 --> 00:03:09.179
here, because I always wanted to work with data. It's funny how life works out, because one of the

00:03:09.179 --> 00:03:14.059
days I was in Lithuania, I was walking around, and I saw Lithuanian Central Statistics Office.

00:03:14.059 --> 00:03:19.979
I was like, I would like to work for this. And chances happen, I end up working for Ireland's

00:03:19.979 --> 00:03:23.979
statistical office. But I keep close relationships with Lithuanian statistical office as well.

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:30.139
Amazing. Is statistics, straight statistics, your background? How did you get here education-wise?

00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:38.139
So actually, my education is quite different. My dream was to get into programming. So that's

00:03:38.139 --> 00:03:44.059
what I actually studied in the university. I studied computers, and my background was programming.

00:03:44.059 --> 00:03:49.100
And then when I did master's, I did it in data analytics and machine learning. That's how I

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:55.419
developed real passion for AI, as I told people way, way back then when AI wasn't popular.

00:03:55.979 --> 00:04:02.380
So now when actually AI became popular, I must say, I got on that train right away,

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:08.619
because that was my passion all along. It just was sleeping there. The moment wasn't quite right.

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:12.860
So yeah, so that's how I began working with data. That's my background.

00:04:13.419 --> 00:04:16.459
And I suppose that's the area where I always wanted to be in.

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:23.899
Well, I'm glad that you got there. Could you tell us a little bit about what CSO does, and give an

00:04:23.899 --> 00:04:30.859
example of the types of data that you work with and see on a daily or weekly basis, just so that

00:04:30.859 --> 00:04:34.859
we can have a good sort of understanding? Because we come from all different backgrounds, whether

00:04:34.859 --> 00:04:40.779
that's biology, working with assay data, or maybe we come from financial institutions where everything

00:04:40.779 --> 00:04:45.739
is transactional. But I think that you see a very specific type of data, or a few types of data.

00:04:46.859 --> 00:04:52.779
Yeah, to be honest, I think it's a very good question, because sometimes, you know, we forget

00:04:52.779 --> 00:04:58.859
an area that we're in. So just to give a little bit of context, CSO is a central statistics office

00:04:58.859 --> 00:05:05.100
for the Irish government. And every government in the world, they have the same, they have a central

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:11.500
statistics office. So what we do, we do public releases on various things, like how many people

00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:16.779
live in the country, which you might know is census. And then we do lots of important economic

00:05:16.779 --> 00:05:24.220
things like CPI, GDP, things along those lines. And if you combine all the releases together,

00:05:24.220 --> 00:05:31.660
CSO as an organization does around 300 releases a year. So on various, various different

00:05:32.540 --> 00:05:39.980
things. And the way we get our data for those releases historically has been surveys. So

00:05:39.980 --> 00:05:46.299
somebody knocks on your door and asks you to complete a survey. That's historically how we

00:05:46.299 --> 00:05:53.179
get the data in. Now, in the recent times, all statistical offices around the world,

00:05:53.179 --> 00:05:59.980
they see that people no longer like to answer doors. It's a different area that we live in. So

00:05:59.980 --> 00:06:05.660
statistics are changing a little bit, but I guess alternative is there. So statistical offices are

00:06:05.660 --> 00:06:12.380
now moving more to administrative data sources, basically data that does not come directly from

00:06:12.380 --> 00:06:17.579
the people. Surveys are still there. There's still like a lot of man in the service that you

00:06:17.579 --> 00:06:22.859
need to do, but unfortunately the responses, response rates are dropping down. People are

00:06:22.859 --> 00:06:28.940
just not responding. So as I say, other data sources are coming in. So we'll work closely

00:06:28.940 --> 00:06:34.940
with other governmental departments, with LinkedIn, with all the governmental agencies,

00:06:34.940 --> 00:06:41.899
and we get data from them, which is transactional level data. In some cases, it's no longer

00:06:41.980 --> 00:06:49.660
a survey when you have complete data sets. So in some cases, it's very good. And we also see

00:06:49.660 --> 00:06:57.339
some very interesting sections set up. I even see in my organizations, geospatial data.

00:06:57.339 --> 00:07:03.899
That's very new. So like in some cases, we have some mandatory statistics that we need to produce for

00:07:03.899 --> 00:07:11.100
sustainable development goals. And one of them is CO2 emissions. Now, question is, how do you

00:07:11.100 --> 00:07:16.299
measure CO2 emissions? You can't ask a person, what's your CO2 emission in your backyard?

00:07:16.940 --> 00:07:22.859
Satellite. Satellite sees that. So I suppose satellite will be one of the most advanced data

00:07:22.859 --> 00:07:29.019
sources, but just to say the variety of data sources that are coming in moved way beyond

00:07:29.019 --> 00:07:34.540
just knocking on the doors and asking for people's information to all these diverse

00:07:34.540 --> 00:07:37.820
data sources, other governmental departments, satellite,

00:07:37.820 --> 00:07:42.700
data, and many others. So I suppose like that is how we work now.

00:07:44.540 --> 00:07:50.940
Okay. Well, we have a fantastic question to get us started in Slido, and it's from Anna.

00:07:51.899 --> 00:07:56.299
