00:08:14.140 --> 00:08:19.179
My question is, what programming languages do you use in your work? And was that the same

00:08:19.179 --> 00:08:23.820
language that you study in school? Very good question. That comes very

00:08:23.820 --> 00:08:30.140
close at heart. No, when I was in school, I developed a passion for Java. I really liked

00:08:30.140 --> 00:08:36.299
that. But to be honest, it set me up for life because I really liked the object-oriented

00:08:36.299 --> 00:08:43.099
way of thinking. But then in practice, once I moved into office, Java wasn't really used.

00:08:43.099 --> 00:08:50.219
Now, in our office, our legacy language for around 40 years, I would say, was SAS.

00:08:50.539 --> 00:08:58.460
But we're moving away from that. And that's actually the whole purpose of my job is to move

00:08:58.460 --> 00:09:06.940
to a more modern language, which is R. But because of that, that's why I built up my section,

00:09:06.940 --> 00:09:13.099
because we can't just say, oh, we want to do R now, which we are doing it. But infrastructure

00:09:13.099 --> 00:09:18.700
and software needs to be built up. And that's what I did in my office. I built up the capability.

00:09:18.780 --> 00:09:25.659
So now we're fully into R. And if history is to teach us anything, the previous language was

00:09:26.700 --> 00:09:32.940
SAS, now R. So we're here to stay. And just to say the reason, because I also did the research

00:09:34.140 --> 00:09:37.979
when I actually began working in my organization, I was tasked with the task,

00:09:38.780 --> 00:09:44.539
go and research what is the best alternative language. And I did the research. And it's

00:09:44.539 --> 00:09:50.460
actually on my source of research that we decided to go to R. Let's just say the only two contesters

00:09:50.460 --> 00:09:57.419
that really came into play was R and Python. But R won it overall. But I see Python bidding in the

00:09:57.419 --> 00:10:03.260
mix. But I say, let's just deal with one language, please. When you deal with official statistics,

00:10:04.619 --> 00:10:10.619
the methodological consistency is very, very important. Just say, as an example, we have

00:10:10.619 --> 00:10:16.219
vital statistics that's actually a time series that goes back over 100 years. So you really

00:10:16.219 --> 00:10:21.580
don't want to mess with the methodology. You want to keep it the same. So that's a long answer to

00:10:22.380 --> 00:10:28.460
a short question. It was a great answer. And I see Chloe in the chat saying that Java was

00:10:28.460 --> 00:10:32.460
also the first programming language that she learned and hasn't used it in a decade.

00:10:33.820 --> 00:10:38.299
I feel like someone else said Java as well. We had someone else who was coming from a much more

00:10:38.299 --> 00:10:44.940
computer science background recently, which I always really love as the sort of transition,

00:10:44.940 --> 00:10:50.140
because I come from the statistics side, where we're all trying to get into the computer science

00:10:50.140 --> 00:10:54.380
side perpetually and feeling like we're missing a computer science side all the time.

00:10:55.659 --> 00:11:00.380
It's nice that there's a mix of us out there. Thank you, Anna, for asking your question.

00:11:01.419 --> 00:11:07.500
We had an anonymous question come up here in the Slido that says, did you have any pushback

00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:13.260
on implementing or growing the statistical analysis team? And how did you convince people

00:11:13.260 --> 00:11:17.580
on the value of statistics? Which might not have been too hard in the literal office of

00:11:18.380 --> 00:11:21.500
central office of statistics, but have you ever had

00:11:21.500 --> 00:11:25.900
any pushback on implementing or growing anything in your area?

00:11:28.059 --> 00:11:33.820
Yeah, the pushback was not major, but I guess from the very beginning,

00:11:34.380 --> 00:11:43.500
the idea was floating there in the ether. I just sort of took the torch and I showed the value.

00:11:43.500 --> 00:11:48.780
Okay, so if we move here, what is the value? And I think that's what I've been doing ever

00:11:48.780 --> 00:11:53.179
since, showing the value. So show them the value, why we're doing this.

00:11:53.820 --> 00:12:01.820
And once we began moving to it, the reason why my team grew so much,

00:12:02.859 --> 00:12:07.260
because the management board, the senior management, well, they've seen the value.

00:12:08.780 --> 00:12:15.099
I never asked for more people. I was always given more people say, you're doing a good thing. We

00:12:15.099 --> 00:12:19.659
want you basically to scale. Some people say, oh, can you duplicate yourselves? Like, nope.

00:12:20.539 --> 00:12:27.659
So therefore we'll give you more people. So it was fairly organic, but there was a little pushback

00:12:27.659 --> 00:12:34.059
every now and then, but my strategy was always show the value, show why we're doing this.

00:12:35.260 --> 00:12:43.099
And therefore the pushback was minimal because that's the general trend within the statistical

00:12:43.099 --> 00:12:47.260
offices. And just to say statistical offices worldwide, they're very, very closely connected

00:12:47.260 --> 00:12:52.700
groups. So I work with many, many other statistical offices across the globe. That's Canada

00:12:52.700 --> 00:12:59.900
